event: response.function_call_arguments.done
data: {"type":"response.function_call_arguments.done","sequence_number":439,"item_id":"fc_08bb6ce65628c391016adb099d681c819186ad044eaefe4848","output_index":3,"arguments":"{\"obstacle_json\":\"{\\\"status\\\":\\\"SUBSTANTIVE_GAP\\\",\\\"location\\\":\\\"proof-draft.json, proof paragraph 2 (localization and matrix-unit argument)\\\",\\\"explanation\\\":\\\"I cannot certify the stated route as a complete proof because the passage from an arbitrary admissible lattice M in the complex module V(λ) to a full R-lattice in the rational span of the standard basis is not justified. M is only assumed to be a full Z-lattice in the complex vector space; its Z-span need not initially lie in the Q-form spanned by e_0,…,e_λ. The subsequent coordinate ideals I_i={a∈Q: ae_i∈M_R}, and the claim that M_R is their direct sum, require first proving that the rational span of M equals the standard Q-form. The draft's assertion that stability under the matrix units supplies that fact is not established: it has already treated the image A_R and its standard matrix units as operators on that rational span, while the argument establishing those matrix units is written in the standard Q-form. Resolving this requires a careful scalar-field descent/localization argument; I cannot treat this as a routine detail without introducing an unsupported identification.\\\",\\\"basis\\\":\\\"The frozen source-context allows an arbitrary admissible lattice in a complex module, not just a lattice in the standard integral form. The matrix-unit formulas establish rational matrices in the standard basis, but do not by themselves define a rational form containing M. The exact passage is needed for every later coordinate-ideal and reduction assertion. Per the authoring rules, I stop rather than silently assume the missing rational-form compatibility or replace the proof route.\\\",\\\"partial_progress\\\":\\\"The displayed highest-weight and divided-power formulas in the standard basis are locally verifiable. Conditional on a valid identification of M localized at p with a full lattice in the standard rational form, the matrix-unit and fractional-ideal arguments would compare its reduction with the standard reduction, after which matrix units prove irreducibility including λ=0 and p=2. No NL0 artifact has been submitted because the gap blocks the target proof.\\\"}\"}"}

event: response.output_item.done
data: {"type":"response.output_item.done","sequence_number":440,"output_index":3,"item":{"type":"function_call","id":"fc_08bb6ce65628c391016adb099d681c819186ad044eaefe4848","call_id":"call_2d1f55d16f004db08aa7811151208cce","name":"report_obstacle","arguments":"{\"obstacle_json\":\"{\\\"status\\\":\\\"SUBSTANTIVE_GAP\\\",\\\"location\\\":\\\"proof-draft.json, proof paragraph 2 (localization and matrix-unit argument)\\\",\\\"explanation\\\":\\\"I cannot certify the stated route as a complete proof because the passage from an arbitrary admissible lattice M in the complex module V(λ) to a full R-lattice in the rational span of the standard basis is not justified. M is only assumed to be a full Z-lattice in the complex vector space; its Z-span need not initially lie in the Q-form spanned by e_0,…,e_λ. The subsequent coordinate ideals I_i={a∈Q: ae_i∈M_R}, and the claim that M_R is their direct sum, require first proving that the rational span of M equals the standard Q-form. The draft's assertion that stability under the matrix units supplies that fact is not established: it has already treated the image A_R and its standard matrix units as operators on that rational span, while the argument establishing those matrix units is written in the standard Q-form. Resolving this requires a careful scalar-field descent/localization argument; I cannot treat this as a routine detail without introducing an unsupported identification.\\\",\\\"basis\\\":\\\"The frozen source-context allows an arbitrary admissible lattice in a complex module, not just a lattice in the standard integral form. The matrix-unit formulas establish rational matrices in the standard basis, but do not by themselves define a rational form containing M. The exact passage is needed for every later coordinate-ideal and reduction assertion. Per the authoring rules, I stop rather than silently assume the missing rational-form compatibility or replace the proof route.\\\",\\\"partial_progress\\\":\\\"The displayed highest-weight and divided-power formulas in the standard basis are locally verifiable. Conditional on a valid identification of M localized at p with a full lattice in the standard rational form, the matrix-unit and fractional-ideal arguments would compare its reduction with the standard reduction, after which matrix units prove irreducibility including λ=0 and p=2. No NL0 artifact has been submitted because the gap blocks the target proof.\\\"}\"}","status":"completed"}}

